#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706398 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(1,-1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}&=0\\\\x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=2 x\\\\g_y&=2 y\\\\g_z&=4 z - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,-1,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right)=2\\\\g_y(P)&=2 \\left(-1\\right)=-2\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) - 4=-4\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla g(P)=2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706862 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x-1)^2+y^2\\leq1\\}$. The disk is shown below.\n\n![Disk of radius 1 centered at (1,0)](images/14/1417d15b6192e34275ab2688f5861233f8a6c337b84d3a7952a31951f88e35ef.png)\n\nIts outer circular boundary has polar equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$ to rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The disk lies to the right of the origin. Rays through its interior have $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2$; the endpoint rays contribute only the origin. Along each ray, $r$ starts at $0$ and ends at the supplied boundary $2\\cos\\theta$.\n\n![Radial segment from the origin to the circle and angular sweep through the right half-plane](images/04/0406772f24681d58c80d68bb8bd82e9cb5b9931c5faad8078c683d5e60283964.png)\n\nThe density is $1$, so including the area factor gives $1\\cdot r=r$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{2\\cos\\theta}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe number $1$ is the radius measured from the disk's center; it is not the polar distance from the origin to every boundary point. Omitting $r$ loses the area factor. A full angular turn includes negative upper radial bounds, and $2\\sin\\theta$ describes a different disk."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706868 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^2+(y+1)^2\\leq1\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe region is a disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,-1)$, tangent to the origin.\n\n![Disk of radius 1 centered at (0,-1)](images/55/557e8cd5bcabc2e0aaa9410be010a37d56682c1c5bd1a9909f27fdb4cdde8c35.png)\n\nExpanding the disk inequality and substituting polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+y^2+2y&\\leq0\\\\\nr^2+2r\\sin\\theta&\\leq0\\\\\nr(r+2\\sin\\theta)&\\leq0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, this means $r\\leq-2\\sin\\theta$; the origin is also included. The rays entering the disk lie below the $x$-axis, so choose $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$. On this interval $-2\\sin\\theta\\geq0$.\n\n![Radial segment and angular sweep through the lower half-plane](images/bf/bf7eb129a2dfa2163083399637b02f06e486966bbd2a88c6824172d873b6297a.png)\n\nThe transformed density and area element are\n\n$$\ny^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=(r\\sin\\theta)^2r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n=r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\sin\\theta}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing $r^2\\sin^2\\theta$ omits the area factor. Reversing the radial sign makes the upper bound negative here. A full turn includes rays outside $D$, and the constant upper bound $1$ would use distance from the wrong center."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706873 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+1)^2+y^2\\leq1,\\ y\\geq0\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe full disk has radius $1$ and center $(-1,0)$. The restriction $y\\geq0$ keeps its upper half.\n\n![Upper half of the disk centered at (-1,0)](images/db/dbe2693400c48eed005fc583951553c1245fbecd133cfcf95c443802ed577b86.png)\n\nThe circular inequality becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+y^2+2x&\\leq0\\\\\nr^2+2r\\cos\\theta&\\leq0\\\\\nr(r+2\\cos\\theta)&\\leq0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $0\\leq r\\leq-2\\cos\\theta$. The full disk requires $\\cos\\theta\\leq0$, which permits $\\pi/2\\leq\\theta\\leq3\\pi/2$. The additional condition $y=r\\sin\\theta\\geq0$ restricts us to $\\pi/2\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$.\n\n![Radial segment and angular sweep restricted to the second quadrant](images/37/37e7ef8fa8e628bb866e176fcfa0b19105d5d738d80b339b45b633246793cf18.png)\n\nTransform the difference in the exponent before including the area factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-y^2&=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\\n&=r^2\\cos(2\\theta),\\\\\ne^{x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r e^{r^2\\cos(2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe full left-half-plane sweep would include the lower half of the disk. On the chosen interval, $2\\cos\\theta$ has the wrong sign. Dropping $r$ omits the area factor, and replacing the exponent by $r^2$ incorrectly treats a difference of squares as a sum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706883 :question/problem "![Shaded disk centered at the origin](images/55/5591e60602a79383a0242dc2f588ee0a4553de5e949c1ae80abaf5821ac0a7e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the shaded disk shown above. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose integrand includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The extra factor of $r$ comes from the area element.\n\nThe circle has radius $2$ and is centered at the origin. A full turn covers the disk once, so\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le2,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\n$$\n\nSubstitute for $y$ and include the area factor:\n\n\n$$\ny^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=(r\\sin\\theta)^2r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta=r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706890 :question/problem "![Shaded quarter-disk centered at the origin](images/2a/2ae1a2cecf6a51569db0beec997904c6ecacf22d4005fb21f89bd7122d6a079f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the shaded quarter-disk shown above. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose integrand includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The extra factor of $r$ comes from the area element.\n\nThe shaded region lies in the second quadrant inside the circle of radius $2$. Its boundary rays are the positive $y$-axis and negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le2,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le\\pi\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ and $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$ because $r\\ge0$. Then use $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin(2\\theta)$:\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}&=2(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)r\\\\&=2r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\&=r^3\\sin(2\\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the area factor $r$ gives $r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706902 :question/problem "![Shaded quarter-disk centered at the origin](images/b4/b4c3a67f9864049dc09e243365c0882f06488ef46075464eec141215c470c6e8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the shaded quarter-disk shown above. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose integrand includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The extra factor of $r$ comes from the area element.\n\nThe shaded region lies in the fourth quadrant inside the circle of radius $2$. Starting on the negative $y$-axis and turning counterclockwise to the positive $x$-axis gives\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le2,\\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le0\n$$\n\nSubstitute and use $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin(2\\theta)$:\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=2(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=2r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707123 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=-1$. Given that $P(-1,2,4)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=3x-2y,\n$$\n\nfind the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane $x=-1$ holds $x$ constant, so $y$ varies along the section. Its tangent slope is $dz/dy=f_y(-1,2)$.\n\nSubstitute the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=f_y(-1,2)\\\\\n &=3(-1)-2(2)\\\\\n &=-3-4\\\\\n &=-7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $z=4$ locates the point on the surface; it is not an input to $f_y(x,y)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707128 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=f(x,y)=12-3x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2$. The diagram shows this section, with $x$ horizontal and $z$ vertical.\n\n![](images/d5/d57e0d9e2dddbbfa44a0545f83aac1e2c4626d552fbb2fbd71983840e498f0d9.png)\n\nFind the slope $m=dz/dx$ of the tangent to $C$ at $P(1,2,5)$.\n\n$m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=2$ holds $y$ constant, so the slope of this section is given by $f_x$, not $f_y$.\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=0-6x-0\\\\\n &=-6x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, $x=1$, so\n\n$$\nm=f_x(1,2)=-6(1)=-6.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using the section itself: $g(x)=f(x,2)=8-3x^2$, and $g'(1)=-6$. The curve descends as $x$ increases through $1$, consistent with the negative slope."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707133 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined, for $y>0$, by\n\n$$\nz=f(x,y)=x\\ln y+\\frac{e^{xy}}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1$. Find the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at the point $P$ whose $y$-coordinate is $2$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=1$ is fixed, $y$ varies along $C$. The required slope is $f_y(1,2)$.\n\nHolding $x$ constant, differentiate $x\\ln y$ and use the quotient rule on $e^{xy}/y$. The chain rule gives $\\partial(e^{xy})/\\partial y=xe^{xy}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y(xe^{xy})-e^{xy}}{y^2}\\\\\n &=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{e^{xy}(xy-1)}{y^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $(x,y)=(1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_y(1,2)&=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2(2-1)}{4}\\\\\n &=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{e^2}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a check, the section is $g(y)=\\ln y+e^y/y$. Its derivative is $g'(y)=1/y+e^y/y-e^y/y^2$, which gives the same slope at $y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707146 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d9/d919a1a51a86933bbe207c280000d3cb2a0af687445ad4f8a327db339715fed7.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 2 \\\\\n0\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{2}- 3 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[- \\frac{3 r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 12 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[12 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(12\\right) \\\\\n&=-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707148 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d0/d04f034896058356f7d978567dad992206e558d0cadad1f56ba3ab430d1b147e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{3}r^{2} \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^{3} \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)}{3}\\right]_0^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(9 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 18 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(-9\\right)-\\left(18\\right) \\\\\n&=-27\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707149 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/cf/cfcf5a05620442b1c529e896e6018540a976d1349c3c942f8b027ef6f757ae7f.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 2 \\\\\n- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\int_0^{2}- r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0} \\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707163 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d0/d04f034896058356f7d978567dad992206e558d0cadad1f56ba3ab430d1b147e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\nFor the radial primitive, set $u=r^2+1$, so $du=2r\\,dr$; its logarithm is defined since $r^2+1>0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{3}\\frac{3 r}{r^{2} + 1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(r^{2} + 1 \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 \\theta \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{9 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707164 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$ where $y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/33/335dde7dc67f1b7f582c8e2f2d40e01271ce83d0f184fc86211c1171d244897e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 1 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_0^{1}- r^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_0^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right]_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707194 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 3 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=-3x \\\\\nr^2&=-3r\\cos\\theta \\\\\nr&=- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le - 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe denominator vanishes only at boundary endpoints. Interpret angular endpoints as one-sided limits. Radial evaluation cancels the apparent singularity, leaving the continuous angular expression below, so the improper integral converges.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}- \\frac{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right]_0^{- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(- \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707196 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 2 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=-2x \\\\\nr^2&=-2r\\cos\\theta \\\\\nr&=- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le - 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe denominator vanishes only at boundary endpoints. Interpret angular endpoints as one-sided limits. Radial evaluation cancels the apparent singularity, leaving the continuous angular expression below, so the improper integral converges.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\frac{2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right]_0^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707199 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $x\\le0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=3 y$, shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=3y \\\\\nr^2&=3r\\sin\\theta \\\\\nr&=3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\nFor the angular primitive use $u=\\sin\\theta$, $du=\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}3 r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[\\frac{3 r^{4} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(\\frac{243 \\sin^{4}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{243 \\sin^{5}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{20}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(\\frac{243}{20}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{243}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707203 :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/4d/4d9d33bee82c23fc4e77f2f665931c7617f07fb5aa3ddbd0f8d09f3451fceecb.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 1 \\\\\n0\\le\\theta&\\le 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_0^{1}- r^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_0^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707220 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)=18-x^2-y^2$ with the plane $x=3$. The point $P(3,2,5)$ lies on $C$, and $f_y=-2y$.\n\n![The surface cut by the plane x = 3, with its intersection curve and tangent at P.](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$.\n\nUse $m=f_y(3,2)$ and $z-z_0=m(y-y_0)$, keeping $x=3$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is fixed, $y$ varies along the section. Its tangent slope is\n\n$$\nm=f_y(3,2)=-2(2)=-4.\n$$\n\nUse the point $(y_0,z_0)=(2,5)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-5&=-4(y-2) \\\\ z&=-4y+13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent remains in the plane $x=3$, so the line is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ z &=-4y+13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs a check, the section is $z=9-y^2$. At $y=2$ its height is $5$ and its derivative is $-4$, agreeing with the tangent shown below.\n\n![The parabolic section in the yz coordinates and its tangent at P.](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707223 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e$. The point $P(e,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=\\frac{\\ln x}{\\sqrt{y+1}}+\\frac{e^y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$. Give exact answers, with $z$ expressed in terms of $y$.\n\n$x=$\n{{plane}}\n\n$z=$\n{{height}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=e$ is fixed, use $f_y$ for the tangent slope. Evaluating at $(e,1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_y(e,1)&=\\frac{\\ln e}{\\sqrt{1+1}}+\\frac{e^1}{e} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply point-slope form at $(y_0,z_0)=(1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2&=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)(y-1) \\\\ z&=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)y+1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)y+1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAt $y=1$ the height is $2$, and the coefficient of $y$ equals $f_y(e,1)$. The equivalent expression $2+(1+1/\\sqrt{2})(y-1)$ is also valid for the second blank."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707225 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-1$. Given that $P(2,-1,3)$ lies on $C$ and that $f_x=y\\sqrt{x+2}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $y$, so the tangent slope is $f_x(2,-1)$, measured as change in $z$ per change in $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=(-1)\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\ &=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $(x_0,z_0)=(2,3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3&=-2(x-2) \\\\ z&=-2x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTogether with the fixed coordinate, the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-1 \\\\ z &=-2x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives $z=3$; its slope is $-2$, as required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707230 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ and center $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2-1y&\\leq0\\\\r^2-1r\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$. Choose $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/4c/4c9c4c04113e0e8c940bca28430d29116ca0273b7523a293bc53560e42d11f4b.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x y^{2}&=r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{5} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{4} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor is missing."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707240 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}\\leq4\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $2$ and center $(-2,0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+4x&\\leq0\\\\r^2+4r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Choose $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/9c/9c6113354c11c94e6ab79576947f853e815bf881778fc155f4ea9255fac8ae29.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\x\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor was squared instead of used once. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707248 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):x^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\leq\\frac{9}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ and center $(0,- \\frac{3}{2})$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+3y&\\leq0\\\\r^2+3r\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2 \\pi$. Choose $\\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq2 \\pi$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/af/af753b03aa012acb3b40fc8b1fe651b21033f2aebf214015f95dd3c6669457e3.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** Use $\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos(2\\theta)$ in the difference of squares. The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2} - y^{2}&=- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\&=r^{2} \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\\\x^{2} - y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in. $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707253 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+y^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4}\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ and center $(- \\frac{1}{2},0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2+1x&\\leq0\\\\r^2+1r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq - \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Choose $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/74/7422e92679515dcce0587cc6b518b259493ca13408d8be7c7f43e0dbcfd6e9a5.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2}&=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in. $\\displaystyle\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was divided out instead of multiplied in."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707260 :question/problem "Let $D=\\{(x,y):(x-1)^{2}+y^{2}\\leq1\\}$. Rewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D y\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSelect the polar-coordinate setup whose bounds cover $D$ once with $r\\geq0$ and whose integrand includes the area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$. The full disk has radius $1$ and center $(1,0)$.\n\n**Radial bounds.** Expanding the disk inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2-2x&\\leq0\\\\r^2-2r\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}&\\leq0\\\\r(r-(2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}))&\\leq0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r>0$, divide by $r$; the origin is included as well. Thus $0\\leq r\\leq 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$.\n\n**Angular bounds.** The upper radial bound must be nonnegative. The full disk permits $- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Choose $- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Every interior point is covered once.\n\n![Region, representative radial segment and angular sweep](images/6e/6e255bbcda42c9a140bea788396762b33063295e40577ae82a62fc95ddcd763d.png)\n\n**Transformed integrand.** The density substitution and area factor give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\\\y\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n**Other choices.** $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: These bounds describe a different disk or part-disk, with a different center direction. The area factor is missing. $\\displaystyle\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{1}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$: The center-to-boundary radius is incorrectly used as a constant polar upper bound. The area factor was squared instead of used once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707274 :question/problem "![Shaded region centered at the origin](images/dc/dc01ad9cc9364f9cedd31909e482e58ffbd6f60d799e3d246d15134f510cb494.png)\n\nRewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates for the shaded region $D$. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose density includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe shaded region has radius $4$. Its polar bounds are\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le 4,\\qquad - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe transformed density is $g(r,\\theta)=f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(r,\\theta)&=((r\\cos\\theta)+(r\\sin\\theta))r \\\\ &=r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required polar integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^2(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707279 :question/problem "![Shaded region centered at the origin](images/49/4968facead4cfd2a5ad1b5fd9dd3483e047bbd5b689328177550aa13cab1d4a1.png)\n\nRewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates for the shaded region $D$. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose density includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe shaded region has radius $\\sqrt{5}$. Its polar bounds are\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nThe transformed density is $g(r,\\theta)=f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(r,\\theta)&=((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)r \\\\ &=r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta) \\\\ &=r^3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required polar integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}r^3\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707285 :question/problem "![Shaded region centered at the origin](images/76/76904d5ed05012b53ed0b0ecdfffd7d71e4f8266fca4e5b0ce3cb07470ba255d.png)\n\nRewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D 2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates for the shaded region $D$. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose density includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe shaded region has radius $\\sqrt{3}$. Its polar bounds are\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin(2\\theta)$.\n\nThe transformed density is $g(r,\\theta)=f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(r,\\theta)&=2(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)r \\\\ &=2r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta \\\\ &=r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required polar integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707289 :question/problem "![Shaded region centered at the origin](images/30/30d99205fe8a27765fb73f868e7a964a581755095261ddceb8aa45c8e31d60d2.png)\n\nRewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^2+y^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates for the shaded region $D$. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose density includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe shaded region has radius $\\sqrt{5}$. Its polar bounds are\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\qquad - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nThe transformed density is $g(r,\\theta)=f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(r,\\theta)&=e^{(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2}r \\\\ &=re^{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)} \\\\ &=re^{r^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required polar integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}re^{r^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707295 :question/problem "![Shaded region centered at the origin](images/5b/5bb0a8fe01c26e5ba9b0112303a2fa4841f1ab07626ca275d33a5a0f59979bbc.png)\n\nRewrite\n\n$$\n\\iint_D xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates for the shaded region $D$. Choose the setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge0$ and whose density includes the polar area factor. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and $\\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta$.\n\nThe shaded region has radius $2$. Its polar bounds are\n\n$$\n0\\le r\\le 2,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe transformed density is $g(r,\\theta)=f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(r,\\theta)&=(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)^2r \\\\ &=r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required polar integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707325 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $y=-1$. The point $P(2,-1,3)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=2x+y.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=-1$ holds $y$ fixed, so $x$ varies and the slope in this slice is $f_x(2,-1)$:\n\n$$\nm=f_x(2,-1)=2(2)+(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nA change of $1$ in $x$ along the tangent gives a change of $3$ in $z$, with no change in $y$. Hence $\\langle 1,0,3\\rangle$ is a tangent vector.\n\nUsing the point-slope equation, the tangent line through $P(2,-1,3)$ is the intersection\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y&=-1 \\\\ z-3&=3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane can be written $3x-z-3=0$. Its normal is $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 3,0,-1\\rangle$, while a normal to $y=-1$ is $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 0,1,0\\rangle$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2\n=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 0&1&0 \\\\ 3&0&-1\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\langle -1,0,-3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIts negative is the listed vector $\\langle 1,0,3\\rangle$. The component ratio is $v_z/v_x=3$; $\\langle 3,0,1\\rangle$ would instead give slope $1/3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707330 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $x=-2$. The point $P(-2,-4,1)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "The plane $x=-2$ holds $x$ fixed, so the slope in the slice is $f_y(-2,-4)$. Evaluating the given partial derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\frac{-4-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-4+(-2))}} \\\\ &=\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\ &=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA tangent vector is therefore $\\langle 0,1,-1/\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$. Multiplying by the nonzero scalar $-\\sqrt{3}$ gives the listed vector $\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle$. Its ratio of $z$-change to $y$-change is $1/(-\\sqrt{3})=-1/\\sqrt{3}$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(-2,-4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=-2 \\\\ z-1&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(y+4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRearranging the second plane gives $y+\\sqrt{3}z+4-\\sqrt{3}=0$. Normal vectors to these planes are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,1,\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$. Their cross product confirms the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2\n=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1&0&0 \\\\ 0&1&\\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\langle 0,-\\sqrt{3},1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nBoth signs of a direction vector are allowed, but the component ratio must be preserved. For example, $\\langle 0,-1,\\sqrt{3}\\rangle$ has slope $-\\sqrt{3}$, so it is not parallel to this tangent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707343 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)=18-x^2-y^2$ with the plane $x=3$. The point $P(3,2,5)$ lies on $C$.\n\n![The surface cut by the plane x=3, with the intersection curve and its tangent at P.](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\nAt $P$, the slope in this slice is $f_y(3,2)=-4$. Because $x$ is fixed, a tangent vector has $x$-component $0$.\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=3$ is fixed, $y$ varies along the curve. The slope $dz/dy=-4$ means that a change of $1$ in $y$ along the tangent corresponds to a change of $-4$ in $z$. Thus a tangent vector is $\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(3,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=3 \\\\ z-5&=-4(y-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe slice $z=9-y^2$ and its tangent are shown below. The horizontal coordinate is $y$; the tangent slopes downward as $y$ increases.\n\n![The yz slice z=9-y squared and the downward-sloping tangent at P.](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)\n\nTo connect this with the plane-intersection method, rewrite the second equation as $4y+z-13=0$. Normal vectors to the two planes are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,4,1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2\n=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1&0&0 \\\\ 0&4&1\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\langle 0,-1,4\\rangle.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives $\\langle 0,1,-4\\rangle$, the listed tangent vector. Either direction along the tangent is valid; its $z$-change divided by its $y$-change must equal $-4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707347 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nEach input coordinate ranges independently over $\\mathbb R$." :question/worked-solution "The input space is determined by the declared input coordinates, even if a coordinate does not appear in the formula. The output must be a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n- $\\mathbf A$ returns a vector with two components, but its input is a triple $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$. Not using $z$ does not change that input space. Therefore $\\mathbf A$ is not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n- $\\mathbf B$ takes the planar point $(u,v)$ to the planar vector $\\langle u^2,0\\rangle$. Both components are real for all $(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^2$. The second component may be zero, and the formula need not depend on both input coordinates. Thus $\\mathbf B$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n- $C$ takes planar inputs but returns a scalar, not a planar vector. For example, $C(1,2)=5$, not a vector with two components. It is a scalar field.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf B$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707357 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nA vector field assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^2$. A formula may have a restricted natural domain." :question/worked-solution "Check both the input points and the output vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ takes planar input points and returns planar vectors. Its square root is real when $x\\geq -1$, so it is defined on the subset\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x\\geq -1\\}.\n$$\n\nA domain smaller than the whole plane is allowed. Thus $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n- $\\mathbf G$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$ and returns vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$. It is a three-dimensional vector field, not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n- $\\mathbf H$ returns planar vectors, but its input is a single real parameter $t$. It describes a parametrized curve, not a vector field with planar inputs.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707367 :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their entire stated domains? Select all that apply. Keep each stated domain unchanged.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x\\geq y\\}.\n$$\n\n$$\nT=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^2:u\\geq v,\\ u+v>0\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Each candidate uses planar input points. Check that its formula gives a real planar vector at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- For $\\mathbf P$, the condition $x\\geq y$ makes $\\sqrt{x-y}$ real, but it does not guarantee $x+y>0$. For example, $(0,0)\\in S$, yet $\\ln(0)$ is undefined. Thus the formula does not define a vector field on all of $S$. It would work on a smaller domain, but that is not the domain stated in the question.\n\n- For $\\mathbf Q$, every $(u,v)\\in T$ satisfies both $u-v\\geq 0$ and $u+v>0$. Therefore both components are real at every point of $T$, including the allowed boundary $u=v>0$. Its outputs are planar vectors, so $\\mathbf Q$ is a vector field on the stated domain $T$.\n\n- The missing $\\mathbf j$ term in $\\mathbf R$ has coefficient zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s,t)=\\sin s\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\sin s,0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThis is a planar vector for every planar input $(s,t)$. Independence from $t$ does not turn a two-input function into a one-input function. Hence $\\mathbf R$ is a vector field on all of $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf Q$ and $\\mathbf R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707398 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}^{2 \\pi} \\int_{1}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, holding $\\theta$ constant. Substitute both radial limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\int_{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}^{2 \\pi} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{1}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}^{2 \\pi} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}^{2 \\pi} 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the upper angular limit and subtract the lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\left[- \\frac{2 \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{2 \\cos^{3}{\\left(2 \\pi \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 \\pi \\right)}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- \\frac{2 \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{3} \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{3} \\right)}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707409 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, holding $\\theta$ constant. Substitute both radial limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2 \\cos^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the identity\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nThen the angular integral is\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2 \\left(1 - \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nWith $u=\\sin\\theta$ and $\\mathrm d u=\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta$, integrate the polynomial in $u$ and substitute back.\n\nEvaluate at the upper angular limit and subtract the lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\left[- \\frac{2 \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{2 \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}}{3} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- \\frac{2 \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{3} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{11}{12}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
